  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「タブノキ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%BF%E3%83%96%E3%83%8E%E3%82%AD",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 110036825,
      "text": "タブノキ とはクスノキ科タブノキ属の常緑高木。照葉樹林の代表種である。別名、イヌグス・タマグス・クスタブ・ヤブグスとも称される。単にタブ（椨）とも。ワニナシ属（Persea、アボカドと同属、熱帯アメリカなどに分布）とする場合もある。タブノキ（タブ）に当てた漢字「椨」は国字で、タブの「ブ」を「府」で表したものである。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Machilus thunbergii」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machilus_thunbergii",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1366579722,
      "text": "Machilus thunbergii, the Japanese bay tree, red machilus, or tabunoki, is a widespread species of flowering plant in the family Lauraceae. It is native to Vietnam, Taiwan, southeast and north-central China, the Korean Peninsula, and Japan. A sturdy evergreen tree, usually 10–15 m (30–50 ft) tall, and reaching 20 m (70 ft), it is used for timber, and as a street tree. Its bark is the source of makko, a powder used to make a mosquito‑repelling incense. It prefers coastal areas, and can handle saline soil."
    }
